# shadcn/ui Best Practices

Apply when using or prompted to use shadcn/ui components.

## Philosophy: Component Source, Not Black-Box Library

Use shadcn/ui as a component source, not a black-box library:
- Prefer composing and customizing the generated component code in-repo.
- Keep components easy to audit, accessible, and consistent with the design system.

## Installation and Usage

- Use the CLI to add components: `npx shadcn@latest add <component>`
- Components are copied into the project (e.g. `components/ui/`); they live in your codebase.
- Do not treat shadcn as an npm dependency to import from; the code is yours to own and modify.

## Customization and Composition

- Customize generated components directly in-repo rather than wrapping or overriding from afar.
- Compose primitives (Button, Input, Dialog, etc.) into domain-specific components when patterns repeat.
- Preserve the underlying structure (Radix primitives, Tailwind classes) when customizing; avoid stripping accessibility or breaking the API.

## Accessibility

- shadcn components are built on Radix UI; preserve Radix props and behavior (focus management, aria attributes, keyboard handling).
- Do not remove or override accessibility attributes when styling.
- Ensure focus indicators remain visible; use `focus-visible` variants when adding custom focus styles.
- Test with keyboard navigation and screen readers when modifying components.

## Design System Consistency

- Align with project design tokens (colors, spacing, typography) in `tailwind.config` or CSS variables.
- Use the project's Tailwind theme; avoid hardcoding colors or spacing that conflict with the design system.
- Prefer `cn()` or similar utility for merging class names; keep variant logic readable.

## Maintenance and Auditing

- Keep component files small and single-purpose; split complex components.
- Document non-obvious props or composition patterns with brief comments.
- When upgrading shadcn/Radix, re-run the add command or manually merge changes; avoid drifting from upstream patterns without intent.

## When to Use

- Use shadcn when building forms, dialogs, dropdowns, tables, and other common UI patterns.
- Prefer shadcn over custom implementations for complex accessible widgets (combobox, date picker, etc.).
- Do not use shadcn for one-off, trivial UI that a few Tailwind classes can handle.
